Give the KV-offload connector a real window to back up a Mamba align-mode
state checkpoint before it's freed, closing (as far as architecturally
possible) the dominant remaining cause of wasted recompute found in
2026-08-22's live/simulated-traffic investigation (see
docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md's "Offload eviction race" section).

Root cause this addresses: in `mamba_cache_mode="align"`, a Mamba/GDN
layer's KV cache is a single running state, not a chain of independently
resumable positions like full attention. `single_type_kv_cache_manager.py`'s
`MambaManager.remove_skipped_blocks()` (the code
`patches/mamba-align-stale-state-queue.patch` fixed the leak in) frees the
*previous* checkpoint block the moment `processed_computed_tokens` confirms
a newer one has superseded it -- by design, usually within 1-2 scheduling
steps of that request's own forward progress. This has nothing to do with
cross-request memory pressure; it happens even completely uncontended.

The problem: when a *later* request (e.g. the next turn of the same
conversation) does its prefix-cache lookup, hybrid models check each KV
group independently (`kv_cache_manager.py`'s `get_computed_blocks_for_
connector`). Full attention's hit can run far deeper than Mamba's, because
attention retains every block while Mamba only ever offers its single
latest checkpoint. When that happens ("hit_diverged"), scheduler.py asks
the KV connector to independently confirm the deeper boundary is safe
before using it -- and if the connector has nothing stored for Mamba at
that exact position (because the block was freed 1-2 steps after creation,
long before any offload could plausibly catch up), the *entire* deeper hit
gets discarded, not just the Mamba portion, falling back to the shallow
boundary every group agrees on:

  scheduler.py:813 -- "if hit_diverged and num_external_computed_tokens
  == 0: ... Reconcile to the boundary every group agrees on."

Confirmed directly with added instrumentation (temporary, this session):
one 20-minute, 3-way concurrent simulated-traffic run hit this reconcile
branch 29 times, discarding 825,344 tokens of otherwise-valid hit (avg
28,460 tokens/event, up to 49,920 in one event) -- almost certainly the
dominant contributor to the multi-million-token waste measured earlier in
this investigation.

`patches/offload-flush-protect-running-request-blocks.patch` (this same
investigation, earlier) already guarantees an *in-flight* store completes
before its block is reused. It cannot help here: there is no in-flight
store to protect, because the checkpoint is freed by the owning request's
own normal progress before a store could ever be created for it. The
window this patch closes is *before* that fix's window even opens.

The fix: `VLLM_MAMBA_ALIGN_STALE_SLACK_BLOCKS` (new env var, default 0 =
no behavior change). When set to N > 0, a superseded checkpoint stays
retained for N extra confirmed-block-boundaries past the existing minimum
GPU-write safety margin (untouched -- same threshold, same comparison,
per that patch's own docstring on why loosening it risked a correctness
bug) before it becomes eligible to free. This gives the connector's
`_build_store_jobs()` (which now runs, and registers for flush-protection,
every scheduling step per the earlier fix in this investigation) N more
steps of real wall-clock time to actually create and complete a store for
that specific position before it's gone.

Bounded by construction: the pending-checkpoint queue's steady-state
depth grows from ~1 entry to ~(1 + N) entries -- never unbounded -- so
this cannot reintroduce the leak `mamba-align-stale-state-queue.patch`
fixed. It is a real GPU memory cost though: N extra blocks retained per
Mamba/GDN group (3 in this deployment) per concurrently-active
mamba-align request.

