UPDATE 2026-08-23 (overnight, autonomous testing): measured. Does NOT
reliably help. NOT wired into micke-start.sh -- leave
VLLM_MAMBA_ALIGN_STALE_SLACK_BLOCKS unset (default 0).
============================================================================

Same 20-min, 3-way concurrent simulated-traffic test (identical replayed
real conversation content each run, `simulate_hermes_traffic.py --threads 3
--max-turns 20`), varying only this setting:

  slack=0 (baseline, earlier session): 29 events / 825,344 tokens
  slack=2, run 1:                      41 events / 1,108,224 tokens
  slack=2, run 2:                      31 events / 876,928 tokens
  slack=8, run 1:                      38 events / 1,002,560 tokens

slack=2's two runs bracket the baseline (41 and 31 vs 29) -- run-to-run
timing variance in which requests happen to overlap is comparable to or
larger than any effect from the setting itself. slack=8 (4x the dose)
shows no improvement either -- if anything, both non-zero settings trend
slightly worse, though within noise. No dose-response signal in either
direction.

Root cause of *why* this doesn't help, traced directly (not just
inferred from the null result): picked a specific HIT_DIVERGED event and
walked its instrumentation back to `on_new_request` -- the shallow
Mamba-side hit (consistently exactly 4 chunks / 3,328 tokens for one
recurring conversation, across every single run regardless of slack
value) was already fixed *before this request even started*. It's
inherited from whatever GPU state survived from an earlier turn, not
from anything this patch's slack window could affect (that window only
applies to a request's *own* in-progress superseding, not to what
happens to a block after the owning request has long since finished).

Checked whether this is `VLLM_PREFIX_CACHE_RETENTION_INTERVAL`-driven
sparse retention (a separate, pre-existing upstream vLLM mechanism that
controls which Mamba positions ever get hash-registered as cache-
discoverable at all, independent of eviction timing --
`MambaManager.reachable_block_mask()` in this same file). Ruled out:
that env var is unset in this deployment, so
`reachable_block_mask` returns `None` (dense -- every block is supposed
to get cache-registered), confirmed by reading the code and checking the
live process's environment.

CONFIRMED, not just inferred (added `MAMBA_FREE`/`STORE_JOB_BLOCKS`
block-id-level tracing, same session): `remove_skipped_blocks()`'s
stale-superseding freeing branch -- the exact mechanism this patch's
slack targets -- **never fired even once** across a full 2-thread,
12-turn-each concurrent run, while HIT_DIVERGED still fired twice in
that same run (43,264 tokens discarded). This isn't a race this patch
is merely too small to win -- the mechanism it delays literally never
ran. Also noticed, tracing one event by hand: the *full-attention*
group's own local hit was equally shallow (4 chunks, not deep) for the
divergent request -- attention has no supersede-and-free behavior at
all, so a shallow attention hit rules out anything mamba-specific and
points at plain content loss (never computed yet by this fresh
server, or evicted by ordinary cross-request pool pressure) instead.

Corrected conclusion: this is the *same* general cross-request eviction
race `patches/offload-flush-protect-running-request-blocks.patch`
(earlier this investigation) targets -- not a Mamba-checkpoint-lifecycle
problem at all. That earlier fix protects blocks with an *already-
registered* store job; it does not (and structurally cannot, as built)
help when no job was ever queued for the lost content before it got
reclaimed. Under heavy 2-3-way concurrent load this deployment's local
GPU pool churns fast -- confirmed earlier this same investigation via
`offload-verbose: REALLOC N block(s)` instrumentation showing dozens of
blocks reallocated in single scheduling steps, and `block_pool.py`'s
`get_usage()` traces showing sustained 70-80%+ pool utilization under
this load -- and a block freed by *any* path is a candidate for
*immediate* reuse by another concurrent request the moment nothing is
registered to protect it.

What would actually work: not "wait longer before letting go" but "never
let go until backed up" -- a synchronous or near-synchronous store
triggered by the *eviction* decision itself (in `block_pool.py`'s
`_maybe_evict_cached_block`/`get_new_blocks`), not by a fixed time
budget. That requires new coupling between `block_pool.py` (which
currently has zero connector awareness by design) and the offloading
connector -- e.g. a callback hook plus a block_id -> (req_id, group_idx,
chunk_idx) reverse index the connector would need to maintain. Real,
substantially larger scope than this patch, touching the exact
subsystem responsible for the original leak, a suspected hang, and a
CUDA illegal-memory-access crash found earlier in this same
investigation (see docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md). Do not attempt
without careful, incremental implementation and the kind of extensive
live-load testing the original leak fix received -- not something to
unilaterally build overnight.

The mechanism this patch adds (a bounded, opt-in retention slack) is
still real, correct, and safe to keep available -- it's just not, on its
own, sufficient to close this gap at doses this deployment's VRAM budget
can afford. Left in place, defaulted off, in case it's useful as one
component of a future fix (e.g. combined with the synchronous-store
approach above) rather than a complete solution by itself.

